blackbox/*.bag filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Occupancy-grid masks are binary. Without this, git's autocrlf rewrites bytes
# inside them on a Windows checkout, which silently breaks the SHA-256 that
# route_assets.py uses to bind route, band and mask together -- the follower
# then refuses to start with a mask SHA-256 mismatch while the bring-up still
# reports READY. This bit us on 2026-08-15.
*.pgm binary
*.png binary
*.pcd binary
# Route assets are hashed byte-for-byte after stripping only a trailing
# newline, so they must not be line-ending converted either.
*.json -text
# The mask YAML is hashed the same way and into the same route identity
# (drivable_mask_yaml_sha256), and it was left out of the 2026-08-15 fix.
# A Windows checkout turns b9df7724... into 3ce5436b... - the follower then
# refuses to start on a mask SHA-256 mismatch, exactly as the .pgm case did.
*.yaml -text
# Every one of these runs on the NUC and nowhere else. bash rejects a CRLF
# script outright - "syntax error near unexpected token `$'{'" on the
# first function - so a tree that reached the chair through a Windows
# checkout, a git archive or an rsync cannot bring the stack up at all.
# The blobs are already LF; this stops any of those paths changing that.
*.sh text eol=lf
